---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
export const prerender = true;
---
<Base
  title="About Sharp AI"
  description="How Sharp AI indexes FIRST Tech Challenge documentation, what it will and will not answer, and why it carries no advertising."
>
  <section class="wrap ab">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>An index, not an oracle.</h1>

    <div class="ab__body prose">
      <p class="serif-lede">
        Sharp AI answers FIRST Tech Challenge questions from documentation that
        has been indexed section by section. It is a retrieval system with a
        writer attached — not a model asked to recall FTC from memory.
      </p>

      <h3>What is indexed</h3>
      <p>
        Today: <a href="https://gm0.org" rel="noopener">Game Manual 0</a>, in
        full, chunked by heading section so a citation lands on the exact part
        of the page it came from. Planned next, in this order: the official
        ftc-docs site, REV Robotics documentation, CTRL ALT FTC, and the FTC SDK
        javadocs.
      </p>

      <h3>What is deliberately not indexed</h3>
      <p>
        LearnRoadRunner is excluded: it documents Road Runner 0.5 and would
        produce confidently wrong answers for teams on current versions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <em>FIRST</em> Game Manual Part 1 and Part 2 and the official Q&amp;A
        are copyright <em>FIRST</em> and are not openly licensed. If and when
        they are indexed, Sharp AI will return the rule number and a link and
        nothing more — their text will never be stored on an answer page or put
        into a model prompt. That restriction is enforced on the server, in the
        code that assembles the prompt, not in the page you are reading.
      </p>

      <h3>Why there are no ads</h3>
      <p>
        Game Manual 0 is published under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY-NC 4.0</a>.
        Non-commercial means non-commercial: this site will never carry
        advertising, a paid tier, or a sponsorship. That is a permanent
        constraint of using the corpus, not a launch promise.
      </p>

      <h3>When it refuses</h3>
      <p>
        If nothing in the index is a genuine match for your question, Sharp AI
        says so instead of improvising. That check runs on the retrieval scores
        before any language model is called, which is also what keeps the
        service inside a free tier.
      </p>

      <h3>Verify everything that matters</h3>
      <p>
        Answers are model-written from retrieved text and can be wrong. The
        source section is shown beside every answer so you can check it in one
        glance. For anything rules-related, the official Game Manual is the only
        authority.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ab { padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem) 0 2rem; max-width: 52rem; }
  .ab h1 { margin: 0.8rem 0 1.8rem; max-width: 14ch; }
  .ab__body { max-width: 40rem; }
  .ab__body .serif-lede { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .ab__body a { color: var(--accent); border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(176,82,44,0.3); }
  .ab__body a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
</style>
